ersisted, never a secret): driven from settled `ApiKeys` results of the active context (a Version read back starts one; an unknown Version starts an unconfirmed one; a failed one records nothing; the old key's Delete read back absent completes it; a rejected Delete keeps it with the reason; an unknown one is reconciled by a complete key inventory read), plus `acknowledgeDeployment{application}` stored once. The reminder is the Inbox item `rotation-<application>` while a key is confirmed and the old one not read back gone. The one-time secret stays on its change result for the context it was applied in: Reveal is offered only while the core holds it; any sheet exit forgets it and a context change drops it | The issued secret must never be persisted (I6) while the stage and timestamps must survive reads, switches and relaunches (I7) |\n55:| P42 | Playground topic examples classify under the topic | The scan result carries `topics{violation, blocked, allowed}` from the service's `topic_violation` flag and `topic_guardrails_details`, shown apart from the detector flags: a matched topic is \"flagged (blocked)\" or \"flagged (monitor only)\", no match under the profile is \"Not detected\", an unreported verdict says so; detectors stay listed beside it. The prototype's local classifier is not carried: verdicts come from the service | The desktop never invents a verdict (DESIGN-V2 §10 gap 2 closed with service data) |\n56:| P43 | C31 live row runs through `xtask live-run` | `live-run --family L03 --subject C31` runs the C31 topic routine inside L03's designation and writes its receipts and run record under evidence/C31/live, leaving C25's receipts untouched (the consumption ledger stays C25's single ledger) | The live row needs the reviewed runner and the owner's designation, not a second harness |\nOAuth credentials. Support scan-only tenants. Management credentials are separate\nand added for administration workflows; a management registration does not\nimplicitly supply scan authentication. Additional scan credential modes must be\nspecified and tested when needed, not inferred from an SDK constructor.\n\n## D014 — Rust application authority\n\nAccepted in interview round 4, Q14.\n\nRust owns tenant context, credential access, request lifecycles, and permission\nto apply configuration changes. React presents drafts, reviews, and results.\nConfiguration review binds the tenant, credentials, resource, and draft; changed\ncontext invalidates it. Harness proposals enter the same operator review flow\nand cannot authorize Apply. Initially preserve the existing Harness project/tool\nrestrictions; expansion would require a separate explicit design decision.\n\n## D015 — Import work, reestablish authentication\n\nAccepted in interview round 4, Q15.\n\nImport selected user-authored work and settings, including saved cases, reports,\n## D058 — UI v2 \"Refined Native\": frozen prototype, P09 extraction plan\n\nAccepted by the owner on 2026-10-26 (\"I accept it as written\"), with authority to implement P09 end to end autonomously and to proceed with C28 while C26 remains in progress; every chunk is reviewed until it scores 9 in every dimension.\n\nThe owner explored fifteen interactive shell concepts on a Claude Design canvas\n(https://claude.ai/artifact/2SEXT1XMHdZkxHvdw8oaYL), selected \"Refined\nNative\", and iterated its prototype (R2App, R2Onboard, R2Harness ×3,\nR2Rationale) through sixteen independent Codex usability reviews (scores 6, 7,\n7, then 8 on rounds 4–16). The prototype is frozen at canvas version 134 and\ncopied verbatim into `docs/architecture/ui-v2/prototype/` with a SHA-256\nmanifest; the canvas page is not changed again. Prototype data is invented and\nis never shipped.\n\nDecisions (grilling session, 55 questions, all recommendations accepted):\n\n1. Extraction is spec, then TypeScript presentation, then Rust contract, as\n   phase P09 chunks C27–C34 (see the chunk index). The first implementation\n   slice is the shell plus the first-run wizard and add-tenant sheet.\n2. The prototype's state model is normative for twelve named invariants\n   (`docs/architecture/ui-v2/invariants.md`) and illustrative for code shape.\n   Every prototype state field is assigned Rust-owned/observed or UI-transient\n   (`state-ownership.md`); nothing goes to browser storage.\n3. D014 holds: secrets enter only through the host secret sheet and vault;\n   an issued API key is disclosed only through the existing `RevealSecret`\n   host path, held for the active context and never persisted; key rotation\n   is the admitted sequence additional key (`Version`) → local deployment\n   acknowledgement → old key `Delete` with readback (no invented grace\n   period or revocation); typed-name confirmation is validated in the core;\n   tenant switch is refused by the core while a Harness decision is pending;\n   write-capable tool calls use the native confirmation sheet.\n3a. Only products with an admitted service are verified, inventoried or\n   changed (Runtime Security, Gateway, Red Team and Model/skill reads).\n   Agentic Identity Security and Agentic Endpoint Security render as \"Not\n   available in this release\" with a dependency gate; no inventory,\n   verification result or mutation is invented for them.\n4. Tokens live in a local `--v2-*` file; Academy variables stay for the\n   embedded learner view. One Rust navigation inventory feeds the native menu,\n   sidebar and palette; ⌘1–⌘9 are menu-defined. Feature ids gain `home` and\n   `inbox`.\n5. The new shell ships behind a Rust-owned `ui_variant` flag and the legacy\n   shell, flag and legacy acceptance scripts are removed in C34. The Academy\n   learner view is embedded unchanged.\n6. The wizard realises D013 (scan key first and only required credential);\n   v1 import is offered on the tenant step.\n7. Verification: Vitest fixtures named after every prototype wrapper board,\n   Rust tests per invariant, native acceptance extended with focus ring, live\n   regions, topmost modal and Reduce Motion persistence, screenshot evidence\n   at 100%/200% in both themes per chunk. Live calls only in C29 (verify,\n   Vulture, reads) and C31 (review apply, Redtail, prefixed) inside the scope\n   designated 2026-10-25. C27 is reviewed at 9/10 in every dimension and the\n   controller enforces it: a `docs_only` chunk has no native preparation or\n   acceptance and refuses owner acceptance at completion and at recognition;\n   C28–C34 follow D034.\n8. Minimum window 1180×720 with the rail collapsing below 1280 wide; toasts\n   persist under Reduce Motion; \"Show the calls\" panel shows diagnostics-\n   redacted wire data; English only in P09; chunks commit on main except C34.\n9. C27 proceeds in parallel with C26 (docs and controller registration only);\n   C28 waits for C26.\n\n\n## D059 — P09 re-cut: port the prototype, one gate per screen at 8\n\nAccepted by the owner on 2026-10-29 after a grilling session (23 questions, all\naccepted; `docs/planning/p09-recut.md`). C32 had taken twenty review rounds\nwhile the built v2 screens never matched the design: chunks had wrapped the\nlegacy feature components in v2 frames instead of porting the prototype.\n\n- The frozen prototype (D058, version 134) is the specification per screen; the\n  canvas is not consulted (it is unreachable from the implementing account).\n- A screen is done when it matches its board side by side at 1440×900 in both\n  themes with real core data, judged by the owner from composites kept under\n  the chunk's evidence; parity is structural (regions, widths, spacing, type\n  scale, tokens, component shapes), content differs, accessibility\n  corrections may deviate and are noted. Parity precedes review.\n- One heavy gate per chunk after that approval (rebuild, native acceptance,\n  the full fixture suite, the Codex review), accepted at 8 under D032/D034; the\n  phase review stays 9 and every chunk accepted at 8 is raised in C44.\n- v2 screens import no legacy feature view; the legacy files stay for the\n  legacy shell until C44 removes it. C33 was committed without a receipt (its\n  core stands; its two screens are superseded).\n- Chunks: C35 shell, C36 Home, C37 Inbox fix-up, C38 Harness, C39 Administration\n  (a: Runtime Security, b: other products), C40 Test Cases, C41 API Client,\n  C42 Academy (prototype chrome around the hosted embed), C43 Wizard re-port\n  over the existing state machine, C44 legacy removal on a branch and the P09\n  review. C34 is superseded. `Subject::LAST_CHUNK` is 44.\n\n## Directions explicitly requested before round 1\n\n- Fresh application implementation; the existing GPUI codebase is primarily a\n  reference point.\n- React presentation, Rust application implementation, and the new Rust SCM SDK.\n- Tauri was initially acceptable if needed and is now selected by D008.\n- cdot-framework informs the backend foundation through D008's selective reuse\n  and architecture direction; individual imports still require evaluation.\n- Plan first, architecture improvement review second, implementation and package\n  qualification after that.\n- Independent numeric review gates for testable chunks; earlier requirement:\n  scores below 9/10 require remediation and another actual review.\n\n## Interview exit and remaining evidence\n\nThe product/process frontier through Q23 is settled; D024/D025 subsequently\nsettle source terms and all four architecture candidates. Continue the requested\narchitecture work from those decisions. The prior extra blanket scope-confirmation\nrequest must not prevent already authorized design/research. G01 still requires\nconcrete interfaces/commands and independent review before implementation.\nExact identity strings, dependency pins, import compatibility, named numeric\nbudgets, and designated live environments require explicit evidence/specification\nat their declared gates. They are not secretly assumed complete or reasons to\nrepeat settled product questions. No application implementation has started.\n\n## D060 — No import from the alpha; this desktop is v1\n\nDecided by the owner on 2026-10-31, in the owner's words: \"i really really really don't want to have an import from v1. in fact, i plan on calling this v1 since the previous was an alpha.\"\n\n- The previous desktop (`/Users/cdot/development/cdot65/prisma-airs-desktop`) is the alpha. Nothing is imported from it: the reviewed import of alpha work (C23: the Import from v1 destination and view, the wizard's \"Used Prisma AIRS Desktop 1?\" box and the tenant step's import link, the first-run import frame) is removed from the one presentation in C44, its native checks C23-N01..N09 are retired without replacement, and the legacy-script map and the C44 cumulative set drop `acceptance_c23.py`.\n- Still to decide the same way, in a follow-up chunk the owner names (proposed C45): the core's alpha-import intents and reader (`import_v1_domain`, the `V1Import*` intents, the host adapters), Settings' file-based \"Import tenant profiles\" and the API Client's \"Import v1 API Client profile\" adoption, which are alpha-import surfaces too; their removal changes contracts and earlier chunks' tests and is not done inside the P09 closure.\n- Naming: this desktop becomes v1 and the previous one the alpha. The rename touches the application identity that lets the two coexist (bundle identifiers, data roots, Keychain namespaces, D001-era identities) and the whole document set; it is its own chunk after P09, not a find-and-replace.\n- The product name shown today stays \"Prisma AIRS Desktop v2\" until that chunk.\n\n# 2. Share Academy presentation through explicit composition\n\nDate: 2026-10-16. Status: selected following owner candidate selection; independent G01 verdict\nand implementation readiness are tracked in planning/status.md. Implements D004,D005,D006,D007,D010.\n\nThe current UI package re-exports web app source. Shell palette/mobile navigation\nand theme persistence require tRPC/Clerk/router context; the authenticated web\nentry also starts learner and manager behavior. A whole-app import would spread\nthat authority into unrelated desktop features.\n\nKeep one Academy-owned presentation implementation. Create explicit provider-free\ncontrols/theme/shell exports while preserving the existing tooling entry. Both\nAcademy web and desktop consume the extracted source. Composition adapters supply\nbrand/navigation/account/header/palette/mobile slots; the shared module owns\nvisual semantics, responsive layout and focus. No universal plugin framework.\n\nDesktop global theme is an app preference stored through Rust, not hosted\nsettings.update. Academy web keeps its learner-account theme behavior. Learner\nnotifications, gamification and account effects mount only in Academy composition.\n\nExtract learner views separately when C05/C06 require them. Retain React Query/\ntRPC hooks behind a transport/navigation provider; type-only learner contracts\nbuild without the Express/DB runtime. Web uses existing HTTP authentication;\ndesktop uses a terminating link into the allowlisted Rust Academy transport.\nTokens stay native. Hosted learning/access/grading/award truth and client-safe\nbundle/draft contracts remain authoritative. Never bundle raw answer content.\n\nDirectly embedding the authenticated web root is rejected because its interface\ncontains unrelated account/manager assumptions. Maintaining a desktop UI copy\nis rejected because appearance and learner behavior would diverge.\n\nTests render shared presentation without hosted identity/query providers, run\nboth consumers' regressions, verify native keyboard/focus/themes, and prove no\nlearner mutation/gamification effect from general desktop screens. Learner tests\ncover native link envelopes/allowlist, account cache retirement and hosted authority.\n\nDependency/source/license pins are extraction prerequisites. This ADR changes\nno Academy checkout, package or hosted registration. See\n[selected design](../architecture/DESIGN.md).\n# 3. Retain native Harness and own scoped identity in Rust\n\nDate: 2026-10-16. Status: selected following owner candidate selection; independent G01 verdict\nand implementation readiness are tracked in planning/status.md. Implements D010,D011,D014–D016,D021–D023.\n\nDesktop-server is already a Rust engine. V1 host workers and its package manifest\nstill require Node/managed CLI. Preserve engine depth and replace those host\nrequirements, rather than rewrite the agent. Source inspection alone does not\nestablish that a newly sealed installed bundle works without the old resources.\n\nNative lifecycle owns sealed executable selection, bounded stdin bootstrap,\nprotocol supervision, process cleanup, scoped grants and protected history.\nReact only presents lifecycle state. Grant/tenant/project changes retire the\nrun; approvals bind actual call items. Preserve existing project/process/tool\nrestrictions, Gateway-only inference/MCP and no engine Apply.\n\nIdentity shares native discovery/HTTP/validation/storage mechanics while retaining\nAcademy learner, Harness inference and MCP grant policies. They are distinct\nidentities with distinct sign-out/refresh/context effects. Use public-client\nPKCE/device flows and a no-redirect native transport; never embed a client secret\nor trust renderer-selected issuer/token destinations.\n\nHost signature/JWKS validation is a deliberate stricter v2 requirement; v1\nomitted it. Validate ID/access token types according to their actual contracts,\nnot by decoding arbitrary JWT claims. Rotation/expiry/sleep/revocation fail closed.\nSame-identity token refresh restarts the one-shot-bootstrap engine and restores\ncompatible in-session history; it re-confirms workspace-write permission and\nnever replays remote/tool effects. Declining workspace-write confirmation keeps\nthe draft/cancels Send without restart. Any changed ContextBinding (including\nlocal access/model/configuration/MCP inventory) starts a new conversation; its\nold history is inspect-only under the new binding. MCP-only token rotation also\nrebootstraps/restores an identical binding, but retains working v1's current\nproject-access confirmation without an SSO refresh or another write prompt.\nNative scope additionally binds per-server MCP grant IDs/catalog revisions absent\nfrom engine equality; re-sign-in/sign-out/removal/revision change retires the\nconversation. Expiry alone leaves inference usable with that non-required server\nunavailable and native calls denied. This continuity is distinct from inspect-only\nimported history. No token or refresh credential enters React, diagnostics, process arguments or\ninherited environment. One-time operator-entered inputs remain transient.\n\nUse independent v2 bundle/data/Keychain namespaces. Protected work is encrypted\nand account/context-scoped; no plaintext fallback. Reviewed import creates a new\nv2 data generation, never modifies v1, transfers no credentials/approvals and\nretains a restartable receipt/rollback generation. Historical conversations are\ninspect-only until fresh-context compatibility is qualified.\n\nTests cross native process and identity interfaces: malformed/oversized bootstrap,\nqueue pressure, wrong context, crash/EOF/graceful and forced cleanup, signature/\nissuer/audience/key-rotation rejection, scoped sign-out, protected histories and\nNode-free installed inventory. Native/live/final signed-byte proof remain separate.\nSee [selected design](../architecture/DESIGN.md) for identity/crypto proposals.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
